CORRECTION to my earlier strand-ps bug idea (20260721T184439Z) — please read this before actioning that one; it was a MISDIAGNOSIS on my part. Verified against disk:

1. `strand-ps` does NOT exist and is NOT called by anything. Not by cld, not by forkterm, nowhere in super/dotfiles/aifabric. It is a name PROPOSED in PR #5's method-graduation trap note ("cld calls $BIN/strand-ps"), which I mistook for a description of current code. So "strand-ps is missing/broken" is a non-bug — there is no strand-ps to be missing.

2. Liveness detection ALREADY EXISTS and is ALREADY /proc-based, done right: `super/bin/strands` has live_cwds() (pgrep -x claude -> readlink /proc/<pid>/cwd), and its help even says "LIVE = a claude session's cwd is in that strand right now (/proc-based, can't go stale)". So the fix I recommended (make liveness process-based not .tty-based) is already the implementation. There is no .tty marker scheme to replace — I invented that; `strands` never used one.

3. Therefore the duplicate-session near-miss was OPERATOR ERROR, not a tooling gap: I checked for a nonexistent `.tty` file and ran a nonexistent `strand-ps`, got false negatives, and wrongly declared aifabric-sessions dead — then forkterm'd a second session in. Had I just run `strands`, it correctly showed (and still shows) aifabric-sessions LIVE.

What actually remains actionable for aifabric (much smaller):
- The dup-session GUARD is still a good idea: `forkterm into <strand>` should consult the SAME live_cwds() logic strands already has, and warn "a live session already has cwd in this strand — raise it? (y/N)" before launching a duplicate. Factor live_cwds() out of `strands` into something forkterm can call (a real `strand-ps`/`strands ps` could be that shared entry point — which is what PR #5 was gesturing at all along).
- The forkterm window-RAISE idea (20260721T185421Z) still stands on its own merits (titles don't identify windows) and pairs naturally with that guard.

Net: don't build /proc liveness (exists). DO consider factoring it into a shared helper + the forkterm dup-guard + raise. Apologies for the noise on the first idea.
